WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
A mouse replaces its intestinal lining every few days.

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.840
How can its stem-cell supply last when individual clones can vanish?

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.220
Watch one crypt, the pocket where new lining cells begin.

4
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:21.863
In this simplified picture, a handful of blue stem cells occupy the crypt base.

5
00:00:23.103 --> 00:00:25.563
The epithelium is the intestinal lining.

6
00:00:27.123 --> 00:00:30.163
Cells need niche contact or signals to keep a base position.

7
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:33.057
Stem cells have two jobs.

8
00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:37.237
First, they make specialized descendants that leave the base.

9
00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:41.137
Second, they maintain a stem-cell pool over time.

10
00:00:42.557 --> 00:00:47.037
Neighbours can fill positions as cells leave, so the pool may persist without

11
00:00:47.037 --> 00:00:48.277
preserving every cell.

12
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.690
Shape or Lgr5 expression alone cannot prove those jobs.

13
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.890
Scientists give one expressing cell a permanent green lineage mark.

14
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.910
Every daughter inherits green, even after it stops expressing Lgr5.

15
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.590
The mark follows ancestry.

16
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:14.070
Suppose the marked cell is only a short-lived precursor, a cell with limited output.

17
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.670
What should happen to green after its descendants leave the lining?

18
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.333
A short-lived clone would fade away.

19
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:32.773
Instead, observed green ribbons persisted for sixty days and contained every

20
00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:33.893
epithelial lineage.

21
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:37.133
A lineage is a descendant family.

22
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:42.413
That is multipotent output: several cell types within one tissue.

23
00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:48.023
But a long-lived ribbon does not mean its first green cell survives forever.

24
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:54.543
The trace identifies a stem-cell population through descendant output over time.

25
00:01:55.803 --> 00:01:59.363
What descendants do, not a marker alone, supplies the evidence.

26
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:06.323
Now give nearby stem cells permanent Confetti colours: green, yellow, red and cyan.

27
00:02:07.663 --> 00:02:13.223
In normally renewing mouse small intestine, Lgr5-high cells divide about once daily.

28
00:02:14.543 --> 00:02:16.183
Most divisions are symmetric.

29
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:21.663
Here that means both daughters retain stem positions, or both leave them.

30
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:25.833
The clones appear to compete for niche-supported positions.

31
00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:28.753
Green expands when red leaves.

32
00:02:29.993 --> 00:02:34.813
Red disappears. Later, cyan pushes into green positions.

33
00:02:36.373 --> 00:02:39.313
Colour shares change while the total pool stays constrained.

34
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:46.007
Repeated replacements erase most colours and expand another until one clone fills

35
00:02:46.007 --> 00:02:49.807
the base. Different repeats produce different winners.

36
00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:55.807
The result is takeover by one clone, not survival of every starting clone.

37
00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:02.170
The number of supported positions stays roughly constrained while clone identities

38
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:07.350
change. Balance belongs to the population, not every division.

39
00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.010
No cell is appointed the permanent winner.

40
00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.970
This pattern is neutral drift: chance competition among equivalent lineages.

41
00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:20.153
So follow descendants over time.

42
00:03:21.473 --> 00:03:26.013
During normal mouse-gut renewal, the pool can make specialized cells while most

43
00:03:26.013 --> 00:03:28.173
clones vanish and one may take over.

44
00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:32.573
The population persists even as its membership changes.

45
00:03:33.893 --> 00:03:37.533
A stable stem-cell pool persists while most of its clones vanish.
